(1) No person shall practice body art procedures without first obtaining a body art certification from the department.
(2) An applicant for a body art certification must be a minimum of 18 years of age and shall demonstrate to the health authority his/her successful compliance with all training, disclosure, consent and educational requirements of this body art regulation prior to the issuance or renewal of a body art certification by the department.
(3) Applicants shall submit a completed application provided by the health authority and pay the applicable fee to the county board of health.
(4) Application for the body art certification shall include:
(a) Name;
(b) Date of birth;
(c) Residence address;
(d) Mailing address;
(e) Phone number;
(f) Photo identification;
(g) Proof of successful completion of an OSHA-compliant blood borne pathogen/universal precautions training and basic first aid/CPR classes given or approved by the department.
(h) Hepatitis B vaccination status—Disclosure. An applicant for a body art certification shall provide the health authority, and shall provide to the owner of any body art establishment in which the applicant intends to perform or in which he does perform body art activity, valid documentation of his Hepatitis B Virus (HBV) vaccination status stating:
1. Documentation of HBV vaccination;
2. Laboratory evidence of immunity;
3. Documentation stating the vaccine is contraindicated for medical reasons; contraindications require a dated and signed licensed health care professional's statement specifying the name of the body artist applicant or employee and that the vaccine cannot be given; or
4. Signed certificate of vaccination declination of HBV as required by OSHA.
(5) No body art certification will be issued without successfully completing a department-approved course in blood borne pathogens/universal precautions and an approved basic first aid/CPR course. The body artist certification shall be renewed every two years and shall be conditioned on meeting the completion of these courses and paying required fees to the department.
(6) Acting within scope of certification: A body artist shall only perform that form of body art that is indicated in the body art certification application.
(7) A copy of the body artist certification of any body artist performing body art procedures within the facility shall be posted in a prominent and conspicuous area approved by the health authority upon entry to the facility as well as in each artist's work area where they may be readily observed by clients during the procedure.
(8) Guest body art certification.
(a) No visiting body artist shall practice body art without a guest body art certification issued by the health authority.
(b) The health authority may issue a seven-day certification to engage in the practice of body art. Such guest body art certification will allow a person to practice body art under the direct supervision of a body artist holding current department issued certification. The issuance of a guest body art certification is conditional upon the following:
1. The applicant has received training equivalent to the minimum training requirement set by this body art regulation. Documentation must be submitted to the health authority;
2. The applicant shall provide a letter of consent signed by a body artist certified by the department, a copy of the body art certification of the sponsoring artist, and a copy of the body art facility permit where the applicant will perform body art; a minimum of ten days in advance of the start date of providing services; and
3. Pay fee determined by the county board of health.
(Ord. No. 19-03, § 1(Exh. A), 4-2-19)

